hadiths[1]{hadithnumber,text}:
"1453","Ali bin Saeed bin Masruq told us, Waki’ told us, on the authority of Ismail, meaning Ibn Abi Khalid, on the authority of Qays, who is Ibn Abi Hazim, on the authority of his father He said: “The Prophet, may God’s prayers and peace be upon him, saw me while he was delivering the sermon, so he ordered me to go to the shade.” And in the report of Ubayd Allah bin Bishr: that the Prophet, may God’s prayers and peace be upon him, He, peace and blessings of God be upon him, said while he was addressing the one who delayed coming: “Sit down, for you have been hurt and have suffered.” According to Abu Sa’id’s report: If he has a need, I will send him Or other than that, he mentioned it to the people, and if he had a need, he told them about it, and he used to say: “Give in charity.” And in the report of Ibn Ajlan, on the authority of Iyad, On the authority of Abu Saeed, in the sermon on Friday: Then the Prophet, may God bless him and grant him peace, said to the one entering: “Have you prayed?” He said: No. He said: “Stand up and pray two rak’ahs,” then he said to the people: “Give in charity.” And in the reports of Jabir in the story of Salik, the Prophet, may God bless him and grant him peace, said: “Did you pray?” He said: No. He said: “Stand up and pray two rak’ahs.” Then he, may God’s prayers and peace be upon him, said: “If one of you comes on Friday and the imam is delivering the sermon, let him pray.” Two rak'ahs.\ All of these reports indicate that the sermon is not a prayer"","" and that the preacher has the right to speak about the matter in his sermon. And the prohibition"","" and what represents the Muslims"","" and teaches them about the matter of their religion."""""
